WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.450
This is the sequence alignment lecture for KB8029, Bioinformatics, given by Petras Kundrotas.

2
00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:17.950
It covers how two sequences are compared and scored, the dynamic programming algorithms that find the best alignment, and how the idea extends from two sequences to many.

3
00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:26.275
This lecture draws on chapters three and six of the Pevsner book, with the topics rearranged so that each one builds on the last.

4
00:00:26.875 --> 00:00:28.400
Four parts.

5
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:36.000
First some definitions — homology, similarity, orthologues and paralogues — and why sequences need aligning at all.

6
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:43.525
Then the two algorithms at the heart of pairwise alignment: Needleman-Wunsch for global alignment, Smith-Waterman for local.

7
00:00:43.525 --> 00:00:49.600
Then substitution matrices: the PAM and BLOSUM families, and how each is built.

8
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.975
And finally multiple sequence alignment, which is chapter six.

9
00:00:53.975 --> 00:01:01.400
That chapter also covers alignment databases, which this lecture does not — read them anyway, they are useful in practice.

10
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.400
Start with homology, because it is the word people misuse most.

11
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.325
Two sequences are homologous if they share an ancestry.

12
00:01:10.325 --> 00:01:18.950
Note "ancestry" rather than "ancestor": ancestor suggests species, and genes also duplicate and diverge within one species.

13
00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:22.000
Homology is a yes-or-no property.

14
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.350
There is no such thing as a degree of homology.

15
00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:28.600
Sequences are homologous or they are not.

16
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:32.675
What you can measure is relatedness, and there are two measures.

17
00:01:32.675 --> 00:01:37.500
Identity is the fraction of positions holding the same amino acid.

18
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.550
Similarity is the fraction holding amino acids with similar physicochemical properties — two hydrophobics, two positives.

19
00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:50.050
Similarity is therefore always at least as large as identity.

20
00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:58.325
Homologous proteins almost always have similar three-dimensional structures, usually with detectable sequence similarity as well.

21
00:01:58.325 --> 00:02:02.275
The threshold for calling homology has moved over time.

22
00:02:02.275 --> 00:02:09.250
It used to be thirty to forty per cent identity; with better methods it is now more like fifteen to twenty.

23
00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:15.800
Both orthologues and paralogues are homologous — in both cases there is an ancestral gene.

24
00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:17.850
Orthologues first.

25
00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:23.800
Every myoglobin in every organism does the same job, carrying oxygen in muscle.

26
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:32.200
The red cartoon is human myoglobin, the magenta one is from sperm whale, and both are a hundred and fifty-four amino acids long.

27
00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.150
Paralogues next.

28
00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:39.650
The human globins are all oxygen transporters, but they are not interchangeable.

29
00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:43.075
Neuroglobin, in light blue, works in the brain.

30
00:02:43.075 --> 00:02:48.100
Haemoglobin delta, in yellow, carries oxygen from the lungs to the tissues.

31
00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:51.425
Epsilon globin works in the early embryo.

32
00:02:51.425 --> 00:02:54.150
Then a question worth pausing on.

33
00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:59.100
Are human epsilon globin and whale myoglobin paralogues or orthologues?

34
00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:04.425
They look like paralogues, because the different globins come from gene duplication.

35
00:03:04.425 --> 00:03:08.950
But they are not, strictly, because they evolved in different organisms.

36
00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:13.900
Which leads to the real question: how do we measure the similarity between them?

37
00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:20.150
It is easy when the two proteins are the same length, as human and whale myoglobin are.

38
00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:23.925
Write one sequence under the other, and the measures follow.

39
00:03:24.525 --> 00:03:31.750
Count the pairs where both sequences have the same residue — the blue ones — and divide by the total number of pairs.

40
00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:38.750
Multiply by a hundred and you have percentage identity, which is how every alignment program reports it.

41
00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:42.500
Now look at the red pairs, the ones that differ.

42
00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:44.750
Some differ more than others.

43
00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:48.600
Valine against isoleucine is two hydrophobics.

44
00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:56.900
Arginine against lysine is two positives, aspartate against glutamate two negatives, serine against threonine two polars.

45
00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:05.150
Those are far closer than, say, charged aspartate against hydrophobic alanine, or polar asparagine against charged lysine.

46
00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:12.675
So split the red pairs into genuinely different ones and merely different ones, and you get the second measure: similarity.

47
00:04:13.275 --> 00:04:16.875
Now the hard case: two proteins of different lengths.

48
00:04:16.875 --> 00:04:19.950
The first problem is what to divide by.

49
00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:24.250
Use the shorter sequence, and every residue gets counted.

50
00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.750
The second problem is worse.

51
00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:30.425
There are many ways to place one sequence under the other.

52
00:04:30.425 --> 00:04:39.100
Here the first residues are lined up, which gives seventeen identical residues out of a hundred and forty-seven — eleven point six per cent.

53
00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:45.350
Line up the last residues instead and identity falls to four point one per cent.

54
00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:50.200
And this is the right answer: twenty-one point five per cent.

55
00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:52.100
How do you get there?

56
00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:57.075
The obvious approach is to try every possible placement and keep the best.

57
00:04:57.075 --> 00:05:00.475
But look at the lower sequence — it has gaps in it.

58
00:05:00.475 --> 00:05:07.175
Allowing gaps multiplies the number of possible placements enormously, and brute force stops being possible.

59
00:05:07.175 --> 00:05:09.450
So we need an algorithm.

60
00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:13.350
Doing this properly is called pairwise sequence alignment.

61
00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:23.500
To summarise: pairwise alignment lines up two sequences to maximise identity and to show where evolution has conserved, inserted or deleted.

62
00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:28.900
The point of it is to judge similarity, and from that, the possibility of homology.

63
00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:32.225
Mechanically it is an optimisation problem.

64
00:05:32.225 --> 00:05:37.975
You need an objective function — a score for any given alignment — and a way to maximise it.

65
00:05:37.975 --> 00:05:42.075
That score is not identity and it is not similarity.

66
00:05:42.075 --> 00:05:54.475
It has three parts: a reward for matching residues exactly, which contributes most; a smaller reward for matching residues that merely resemble each other; and a penalty for opening and extending gaps.

67
00:05:55.075 --> 00:05:59.050
So the algorithm must find the alignment with the highest score.

68
00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:03.250
The first question with any problem is whether brute force will do.

69
00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:06.400
Try it with two sequences of length three.

70
00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:11.225
With no gaps allowed there is exactly one alignment, since the lengths match.

71
00:06:11.225 --> 00:06:19.775
Allow one gap and there are twelve more: four places to put a gap in the first sequence, and for each, three places in the second that do not collide.

72
00:06:19.775 --> 00:06:24.050
Four times three is twelve, plus the ungapped one makes thirteen.

73
00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:32.025
Allow two gaps in the first sequence and there are ten ways to place them, each with three ways to place one in the second — thirty more.

74
00:06:32.025 --> 00:06:34.600
Three gaps gives twenty more.

75
00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:38.825
So sequences of length three admit sixty-three alignments.

76
00:06:38.825 --> 00:06:44.625
You cannot go further, because four gaps would put gaps opposite each other, which is not allowed.

77
00:06:44.625 --> 00:06:47.550
There is a formula for the general case.

78
00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:51.000
And the numbers it gives settle the question.

79
00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.050
Brute force is out.

80
00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:58.400
So we need algorithms that find the best alignment without trying everything.

81
00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:01.375
These are the ones this lecture covers.

82
00:07:01.975 --> 00:07:04.900
First, the simplest: the dot plot.

83
00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:07.925
Put one sequence along each axis.

84
00:07:07.925 --> 00:07:11.875
Wherever the residues at two positions match, put a dot.

85
00:07:11.875 --> 00:07:14.150
That is the whole method.

86
00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:17.250
What makes it useful is what the dots form.

87
00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:23.175
A diagonal run of dots is a stretch of similarity — a conserved domain, or a repeat.

88
00:07:23.175 --> 00:07:28.725
Dots off the main diagonal suggest an insertion or a deletion has shifted things.

89
00:07:28.725 --> 00:07:32.175
Repeated diagonals mean repeated elements.

90
00:07:32.175 --> 00:07:40.750
It is a way of seeing the relationship before you compute anything about it, and it works best alongside the methods that follow rather than instead of them.

91
00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:58.200
Now dynamic programming, which is the engine inside a remarkable number of programs: BLAST and FASTA for database search, ClustalW for multiple alignment, HMMER for profile search, GENSCAN for gene finding, even MFOLD for RNA folding.

92
00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:00.525
A small piece of history.

93
00:08:00.525 --> 00:08:08.850
Dynamic programming was formalised in the early nineteen-fifties by Richard Bellman at RAND, working on optimal decision processes.

94
00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:11.400
The name was chosen for cover.

95
00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:19.400
His funder was a defence secretary hostile to mathematical research, and Bellman wanted something that sounded unobjectionable.

96
00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:27.225
His work involved time series and planning, hence dynamic and programming — nothing to do with computer programming at all.

97
00:08:27.225 --> 00:08:33.800
He liked dynamic because, as he put it, it is impossible to use the word in a pejorative sense.

98
00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:36.850
The way to understand it is an example.

99
00:08:36.850 --> 00:08:42.575
Two sequences, x and y, at the top of the slide: thirteen residues and twelve.

100
00:08:42.575 --> 00:08:44.950
The method has two steps.

101
00:08:44.950 --> 00:08:51.525
First fill a matrix, using a gap penalty — one, here — and a score for aligning each pair of residues.

102
00:08:51.525 --> 00:08:55.525
Then recover the alignment by tracing back through the matrix.

103
00:08:55.525 --> 00:09:00.625
Notice the matrix has an extra row and an extra column, numbered zero.

104
00:09:01.225 --> 00:09:11.375
Those extra ones get filled first, with the gap penalty multiplied by how far along you are: by the column number for the top row, by the row number for the first column.

105
00:09:11.975 --> 00:09:15.125
Which, for this example, gives these numbers.

106
00:09:15.725 --> 00:09:18.675
Here are the rules for everything else.

107
00:09:18.675 --> 00:09:21.500
Any cell can be reached three ways.

108
00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:26.475
Diagonally from the upper left, adding the score for matching those two residues.

109
00:09:26.475 --> 00:09:30.075
From directly above, subtracting the gap penalty.

110
00:09:30.075 --> 00:09:33.450
Or from the left, subtracting the gap penalty.

111
00:09:33.450 --> 00:09:36.500
Work out all three and keep the largest.

112
00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:40.725
That is the whole recurrence, and it is the equation on the slide.

113
00:09:41.325 --> 00:09:44.825
Here is that rule applied to the very first cell.

114
00:09:45.425 --> 00:09:47.825
And with the actual numbers.

115
00:09:47.825 --> 00:09:52.000
The largest of the three is two, and it came from the upper left.

116
00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:56.375
So two goes in the cell, and we remember the direction: diagonal.

117
00:09:56.975 --> 00:10:00.000
The rule again, for the cell to its right.

118
00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:02.350
And the numbers.

119
00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:10.225
This time the largest came from subtracting the gap penalty from the cell on the left, so the direction we remember is: left.

120
00:10:10.825 --> 00:10:14.325
Carry on in the same way until the matrix is full.

121
00:10:14.925 --> 00:10:17.000
Which looks like this.

122
00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:19.725
Now the second step, the traceback.

123
00:10:19.725 --> 00:10:23.375
For Needleman-Wunsch it starts in the bottom right corner.

124
00:10:23.975 --> 00:10:27.050
Say the direction recorded there was left.

125
00:10:27.050 --> 00:10:31.250
That means aligning the M from the first sequence against a gap.

126
00:10:31.250 --> 00:10:36.800
The alignment starts to build at the bottom of the slide, and we move one cell to the left.

127
00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:47.200
In that cell the direction was diagonal, which means aligning the twelfth residue of the first sequence against the twelfth of the second — both proline, as it happens.

128
00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:50.925
That pair joins the alignment and we move diagonally.

129
00:10:51.525 --> 00:10:57.625
Here the direction was above, which means a gap in the first sequence against the glycine in the second.

130
00:10:57.625 --> 00:11:00.675
That goes into the alignment and we move up.

131
00:11:01.275 --> 00:11:04.025
And so on until the top left.

132
00:11:04.025 --> 00:11:09.675
The alignment at the bottom is the result, and the number in the bottom right corner is its score.

